-- nucleus's gatehouse pipeline: the six gates, what each one needs, and the machine that runs
-- them. `cargo run -p gate -- plan check` elaborates this and the writ kernel checks the
-- admissibility certificate at the bottom; the gate definitions in `.gatehouse/gates/*.json`
-- carry the same commands, scopes and capabilities, plus the fields a writ Gate has no room for
-- (cwd, resources, falsifier, the whole Env).
--
-- EDITING THIS FILE DOES NOT CHANGE WHAT THE LANE RUNS. Three separate steps stand between a
-- commit here and a gate behaving differently, and 2026-09-22 spent hours discovering each:
--
--   1. The controller serves a plan UPLOADED to it (`PUT /v1/<tenant>/plan`), not read from the
--      tree under test. Landing a change here leaves the lane on the old plan.
--   2. A gate job binds its recipe at ENQUEUE. An upload does not retrofit queued work —
--      measured: a job claimed twenty minutes after an upload still ran the old definition.
--      Re-request the branch (a new commit) so its jobs are created under the new plan.
--   3. The receipt binding verifies the `plan` field, so every receipt produced under the
--      previous plan hash comes back `refuted` and the roll-up can use none of it. Batch plan
--      changes; each upload discards all gate work in flight.
--
--   4. A REFUTED receipt does not re-enqueue its gate. Measured: five gates stuck on
--      `refuted: Binding { field: "plan" }` for thirty minutes, and `POST .../dispatch`
--      answered `gates_enqueued: 1` — only the gate with no scope hash. A job already exists
--      for that (gate, tree), so nothing creates a replacement, and the old binding is
--      permanent. Only a NEW TREE makes fresh (gate, tree) pairs. Together with (3) this is
--      the trap: changing the plan mid-flight does not merely discard work, it leaves the
--      affected gates unable to recover without a new commit.
--
--   5. A NEW COMMIT IS NOT A NEW TREE. (4)'s escape is a new tree, and the roll-up keys on the
--      TREE — so `git commit --allow-empty` does nothing: it makes a new SHA over an identical
--      tree, the same (gate, tree) pairs resolve, and the same refuted jobs come back. Measured
--      the hard way, twice on one branch, before checking `git rev-parse <sha>^{tree}` and
--      finding all three commits shared one tree. A re-request needs a real content change.
--
--      The corollary is a race worth avoiding: push, THEN upload, and a job enqueued in the
--      seconds between them binds the old plan and cannot recover without another tree change.
--      `ci-spec` on this branch was caught exactly that way.
--
-- The cost of not knowing this: a REQUIRED gate (`test-audit`) sat unexecuted indefinitely
-- because the served plan carried six gates while this file declared seven, and nothing compares
-- the two. `GET /v1/<tenant>/plan` is how you find out; there is no gate for it.
--
-- Tuples follow gatehouse's prelude/ci.writ:
--   Cap    = (net, exec, wallMs, cpuMs, memMb, fsRead, fsWrite, secrets)
--            net tag 3 0 = None, exec tag 3 1 = Scoped
--   Gate   = (name, hash, scope, env, cap, cmd, timeoutMs, outputs, image, tools, diskMb, memMb,
--             platform, reads, seeds, measuredMs, replaces, steps, pins)   hash is filled by the elaborator
--   Pin    = (platform, image)
--   Policy = (ceiling, required, queueBudgetMs, maxGates, inFlight, catalogKeep, minFreeMb,
--             retired, implTools, undecidedLimitMs)
--
-- WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. Every one is measured on the builder that runs these gates
-- (`gatehouse-nucleus-acceptance`, 2026-09-17) or read out of a file that is itself pinned. They
-- are not estimates, and a wrong one is a refusal at plan check rather than a surprise in a
-- microVM:
--   text-gates      timeout 1800000, measured 713000. MEASURED AS ELEVEN PODS, which is what it
--                   now is. The old pair (120000 against 26000) was measured when this gate was
--                   ONE pod running eleven greps, and the conversion to steps left the numbers
--                   behind: every step pays a microVM boot, so eleven checks worth 8.2 s of
--                   `grep` cost 713 s of pods. The gate never produced a verdict after the
--                   conversion and could not have — `timeout_s = 120` against a 713 s run — and
--                   nothing noticed because the other five gates were failing for other reasons
--                   at the same time. Measured over 87 step timings on the builder: 86.8 s mean
--                   per step, of which 26.3 s is the check and the rest is the pod.
--
--                   THE RATIO IS THE FINDING, not the number. 87x overhead says the per-step pod
--                   is the wrong granularity for a gate whose checks are cheap; `test-core` at
--                   two steps of real work is the shape steps are for. Collapsing these eleven
--                   into one step that runs a repository script would cost ~87 s instead of 713,
--                   at the price of naming which check failed. That is a design call and this is
--                   not it: this only makes the declaration true of the machine.
--   platform        linux/amd64, all six gates, since 2026-09-30: the lane is an x86_64 VM and
--                   the arm64 Mac lane is stopped. Four of these gates were moved from
--                   linux/amd64 to linux/arm64 BY HAND in #2952, and lean-build was left behind
--                   at linux/amd64 because it does not run in the lane. Nothing compared either
--                   value to the builder, which is what `platformMatches_b` is for: the field was
--                   correct in four places by review and wrong in the fifth by the same process.
--                   It is ONE `let` now, read by every gate, so the move back is one edit too.
--                   `platform` is inside every gate's env digest and so inside its `gate_def`:
--                   moving it re-identifies every gate, and no arm64 receipt answers for an
--                   x86_64 gate (gatehouse#208 refuses the job and the seal on the wrong machine).
--                   The measurements below (measuredMs, memMb, diskMb) are STILL the arm64 lane's;
--                   the first contended x86_64 runs are the measurement that replaces them.
--   queueBudgetMs   21600000 = the 360-minute check timeout in `ci/merge-queue.toml`, which is
--                   the live ruleset value that `cargo xtask ci-spec live-parity` holds it to.
--   inFlight        2: `max_entries_to_build = 1` plus the pull-request head under test.
--   builders        2. One Mac VM, but TWO gate-worker services on it, each running one gate
--                   at a time -- observed repeatedly on 2026-09-22 with two gates in flight
--                   together, and `gatehouse_live_workers` reporting 2. It was declared 1, which
--                   understated the machine and left no room for a correct test-audit. They share
--                   one launch lock, so 2 is the count of workers and not a claim of perfect
--                   parallelism.
--   lanes           2: a node build and a plan gate share the host, queued on one launch lock.
--   hostDiskMb      197316, hostMemMb 23981: `df -m /` and `/proc/meminfo` on the builder. The
--                   disk was 78284 until 2026-09-17, when four pull requests dispatched at once
--                   and the two lanes drove free space between 165 MiB and 3.3 GiB while each
--                   preparation wanted ten. The Mac hosting it had 266 GiB free and the instance
--                   disk is sparse, so the builder was small for no reason: `limactl edit --disk
--                   200` and the guest grew its own partition on the next boot. 134 GiB free.
--   builderMemMb    8192: the worker's `build_memory_mib`, which the executor CLAMPS a gate's
--                   request into rather than refusing it. lean-build declared 16384 — twice what
--                   any lane can give — and would have run in half what it asked for without
--                   saying so. Measured instead: it builds in 8192 in 2m25s, so 8192 is what it
--                   declares.
--   catalogKeep 3, preparedMb 4096, minFreeMb 10240: the worker's retention policy, and the
--                   measured size of one prepared input (4058 MiB).
--   diskMb          what the gate holds on the builder: its selection, its scratch and its share
--                   of the image. The largest scratch measured for a gate was clippy's, 2.0 GiB.
--
-- The same for `sdks/verifier-js`: wasm-pack writes `pkg/` and `target/` under it, and a scope of
-- `sdks/verifier-js/**` swallowed both. Three gates build that SDK, so three of them were writing
-- into the content their receipts bind.
--
-- lean-build's scope lists its SOURCES, not `crates/portcullis-core/lean/**`. Lake writes its
-- build output to `.lake` underneath that directory, so `**` put the output inside the content
-- the receipt binds: the gate wrote into what it had just verified. `writesOutsideScope_b`
-- refuses it, and refused this plan until the scope said what the gate actually reads.
--
-- THE THREE COMPILING GATES ARE RESTORED FROM A PINNED SEED, and on x86_64 there are two.
-- `test` names `main-e8cb8d499-x86_64-t@sha256:fb0c1c30…` (the `nextest --no-run` target
-- directory, plus the verifier SDK's own units and wasm-opt memo under `target/verifier-js-sdk`,
-- at e8cb8d499: main 14be30408 with #3104, whose step 0 builds there); `clippy` and `ci-spec` name
-- `main-e8cb8d499-x86_64-l@sha256:ca42d851…` (both clippy passes, the xtask assurance run, and
-- the same SDK units). They replaced the pair minted at 3c2fa1ba3 (`6ae7c446…`, `4d0515d2…`),
-- which predate step 0's units living in the target dir, and before them 79d4c1a90
-- (`b0151779…`, `3014ca35…`). One union seed served all three on
-- arm64 (`main-ebebf53cd-u@sha256:e8191022…`, which gatehouse#208 now refuses: a seed carries the
-- machine it was minted on and that one names none). On x86_64 the seeds stay split by what each
-- gate compiles even though gatehouse#214 sizes an image to its contents: every placement clones
-- and hashes the whole seed, and clippy and ci-spec would otherwise pay for 7 GiB of test units
-- they never read. Each
-- is a scratch holding the cargo target directory those compile steps produce, stamped at 2010
-- and carrying a manifest of
-- the sources it was built from (`.gatehouse/seed-sources.sha256`). A gate dates each of its
-- own sources by CONTENT against that manifest, so an unchanged workspace crate is served from
-- the seed and only what the diff touches recompiles (it replaced `warm@sha256:f84b9b68…`,
-- which predates the manifest and rebuilt every workspace crate at every tree). Compilation was measured at 88% of gate time and these
-- three are ~1300 s of the lane's ~1450 s of work, so a cold `cargo` was the lane's whole cost.
-- The seed changes how long a gate takes and not what it concludes, which is why `GateSemantics`
-- excludes it: `gate expect` gives test-core the same `gate_def` aae31189f8… with the seed and
-- without, so refreshing a stale cache does not invalidate a receipt on any open pull request.
-- What keeps that honest is `ci.seedPinned_b` — the policy lists the seeds an operator reviewed,
-- a gate may name only those, and the digest is checked against the bytes before a VM boots
-- (since gatehouse #81 that list is the lane's attested `ci.Deployment.seeds`, not the policy's:
-- the x86_64 lane's must hold both seeds above, and its store must hold their bytes). The
-- digest also lands in the pod's `data_digest`, which IS inside the identity the execution signs,
-- so a verdict names the cache it was produced against even though the gate's identity does not
-- move. `fmt` and `text-gates` name no seed because they do not compile; `lean-build` still needs
-- its own (a Mathlib olean cache, pinned by digest) before it can run with no network.
--
-- READS IS DECLARED EMPTY, AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS NOTHING. `reads` is empty
-- because the scan that resolves `include_str!`/`include_bytes!` to repository paths is not
-- written yet, so `scopeClosed_b` holds vacuously here — the clause is in place and its operand
-- is not, exactly as `tools` was for one release. nucleus has 108 such sites; three of them cost
-- a full gate run each to find this week.
import "sha256:876b91c3d19dac6bd07c8baa88f7d41eac1a50b206a80d269be17f7c7f5289c5" as ci

-- The rust:1.96.1-slim-bookworm image index digest (rust-toolchain.toml pins 1.96.1). The gate
-- tools layer the builder runs is built FROM this base and pinned separately, by image id, in the
-- operator's gate-tools manifest.
let env : Digest = #d"e18a79fc84dfcfc3ab5ba72290398a644c135c97eaa881447fddc354ee4701a3"
let image : Bytes = #b"docker.io/library/rust@sha256:e18a79fc84dfcfc3ab5ba72290398a644c135c97eaa881447fddc354ee4701a3"
let platform : Bytes = #b"linux/amd64"

-- THE IMAGE EACH LANE RUNS THE GATES IN, one per platform (gatehouse#201, `ci.Pin`). `image` above is
-- the rust BASE the gate-tools layer is built FROM; these are the layers themselves, one per
-- architecture, each built from gatehouse's docker/Dockerfile.nucleus-gate-tools and pinned by
-- the digest the lane's docker reports for it. An image is built for one platform, so the arm64
-- lane and the x86_64 lane cannot share one: `ci.toolsPinned_b` holds each lane to ITS pin, and
-- `ci.pinsCoherent_b` keeps the gate's own `platform` among its pins. The pins do NOT widen where a
-- gate runs: `ci.platformMatches_b` is an equality with `platform` (gatehouse#206 as merged), so
-- these gates run on the x86_64 lane alone, in `toolsAmd64`. `toolsArm64` stays pinned and
-- reviewed so the move back is the one edit to `platform` above.
let toolsArm64 : Bytes = #b"gatehouse/nucleus-gate-tools@sha256:83265724d7bb1af70cc1377229f6840f84029efddb66438a94c28c278450297c"
let toolsAmd64 : Bytes = #b"gatehouse/nucleus-gate-tools@sha256:1a4332229194f85209b899fad44069680d409c9982aa25e7e74ed43dd9ce5628"

-- The ceiling dominates every gate capability pointwise. It carries `**`, because `test` reads
-- the whole tree — it runs the workspace's tests, and a test that opens a fixture two directories
-- up is not a scope violation, it is a test — AND the union of every other gate's patterns,
-- because `globSubsumes` has no top: it derives `crates/writ/** ⊑ crates/**` and it does NOT
-- derive `crates/** ⊑ **`. The rule is sound and deliberately incomplete, so a ceiling of `[**]`
-- alone dominates test-core and nothing else. Measured, not assumed: `bounded_b` refused this
-- plan until the union was listed.
let ceiling : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 3600000, 3600000, 16384, [#b"**", #b".sandbox-unpinned-ceiling", #b"PolicyManifest.toml", #b"docs/**", #b"Cargo.toml", #b"Cargo.lock", #b"rust-toolchain.toml", #b".cargo/config.toml", #b"clippy.toml", #b"crates/**", #b"tests/**", #b"benchmarks/**", #b"scripts/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.toml", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.lock", #b"sdks/verifier-js/package.json", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.js", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.d.ts", #b"sdks/verifier-js/src/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/test/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/tests/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/demo-fixtures/**", #b"examples/independent-conformance/**", #b"examples/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.toml", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.lock", #b"sdks/verifier-js/package.json", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.js", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.d.ts", #b"sdks/verifier-js/src/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/test/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/tests/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/demo-fixtures/**", #b".github/workflows/**", #b"ci/**", #b"scripts/check-ci-spec.sh", #b"docs/**", #b"docs/assurance/**", #b"kani-divergence.toml", #b"sandbox-trusted-base.txt", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/**", #b".gatehouse/**", #b".cargo/**", #b".config/**", #b".env", #b".ssh", #b"lib.rs", #b"main.rs", #b"profiles/**"] : Bytes, [#b"target/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/pkg/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/target/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/.lake/**", #b"crates/*/src/lib.rs", #b"crates/*/src/main.rs", #b"crates/*/src/bin/*"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)

-- One capability per gate, because hermeticity is `capFsRead ⊆ scope` and the scopes differ.
--
-- ci-spec reads `scripts/**` and `docs/assurance/**` because it runs three commands, not one: its
-- own check, the assurance ledger's script and `xtask assurance-required`. Extending the command
-- without extending the scope is what `scopeClosed_b` is for, and it is the clause whose operand
-- is not written yet -- so the gate found it the slow way, with
-- `scripts/check-ci-assurance-ledger.sh: not found` inside a microVM.
let fcap : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 600000, 600000, 4096, [#b"Cargo.toml", #b"Cargo.lock", #b"rust-toolchain.toml", #b"crates/**", #b"tests/**", #b"benchmarks/**", #b"scripts/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.toml", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.lock", #b"sdks/verifier-js/package.json", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.js", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.d.ts", #b"sdks/verifier-js/src/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/test/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/tests/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/demo-fixtures/**"] : Bytes, [#b"target/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/pkg/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/target/**"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)
let ccap : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 3000000, 3000000, 8192, [#b"Cargo.toml", #b"Cargo.lock", #b"rust-toolchain.toml", #b".cargo/config.toml", #b"clippy.toml", #b"crates/**", #b"tests/**", #b"benchmarks/**", #b"scripts/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.toml", #b"sdks/verifier-js/Cargo.lock", #b"sdks/verifier-js/package.json", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.js", #b"sdks/verifier-js/index.d.ts", #b"sdks/verifier-js/src/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/test/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/tests/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/demo-fixtures/**", #b"examples/independent-conformance/**"] : Bytes, [#b"target/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/pkg/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/target/**"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)
let lcap : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 3600000, 3600000, 16384, [#b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/*.lean", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/lean-toolchain", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/lake-manifest.json", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-attenuation/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-cap-quantale/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-certchain/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-channel/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-credential/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-decide/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-declass/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-egress/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-identity/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-ifc/**", #b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/generated-mediation/**"] : Bytes, [#b"crates/portcullis-core/lean/.lake/**"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)
let scap : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 1200000, 1200000, 4096, [#b"Cargo.toml", #b"Cargo.lock", #b"rust-toolchain.toml", #b".github/workflows/**", #b"ci/**", #b"crates/**", #b"scripts/**", #b"docs/assurance/**", #b".gatehouse/**"] : Bytes, [#b"target/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/pkg/**", #b"sdks/verifier-js/target/**"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)

-- The eleven text gates read the tree and write nothing: `fsWrite` is empty because the checks
-- are greps, and a gate that declares a write it does not make is a claim nobody checks.
--
-- The wall clock moved 120000 -> 1800000 with the gate's timeout, because `timeoutCoherent_b`
-- holds a gate's timeout under its own capability: a gate permitted less time than it declares
-- is a plan that contradicts itself, and the clause refused this until the two agreed. The
-- CEILING did not move — it was already 3600000 — so this capability is still dominated and
-- `bounded_b` holds unchanged. The permission grew because the work did, and that is the whole
-- of it: eleven pods where there was one.
let gcap : ci.Cap = (tag 3 0, tag 3 1, 1800000, 1800000, 2048, [#b".github/workflows/**", #b".sandbox-unpinned-ceiling", #b"crates/**", #b"docs/**", #b"kani-divergence.toml", #b"sandbox-trusted-base.txt", #b"scripts/**", #b"tests/**"] : Bytes, [] : Bytes, [] : Bytes)

