let pipeline : ci.Pipeline = ([fmt, clippy, testNode, testLibs, leanBuild, ciSpec, textGates] : ci.Gate, [(1, 0), (2, 0), (3, 0)] : ci.Edge)

-- `required` is the five gates `gatehouse/required` rolls up, which is what branch protection
-- actually holds a merge to. lean-build is not among them until its toolchain is in the image and
-- its Mathlib cache is a pinned seed.
--
-- Every timeout is at least twice the LONGEST run observed in the lane, which is what makes the
-- capacity figure mean something. `timeoutMeasured_b` holds each to between two and ten times
-- its declared measurement, and the declaration is meant to be the WORST run seen HERE -- "a
-- worst that a later run beats is not a measurement, it is a hope", as the previous revision of
-- this comment put it while being beaten by later runs on all five gates.
--
-- 2026-09-21: re-measured from the builder's own `gate_measured` events, 519 of them in
-- /var/log/gatehouse/gates.log. Every figure below had been overtaken:
--
--            declared    observed worst    new timeout    timeout / worst
--   fmt         123 s          207 s           450 s          2.16x
--   ci-spec      92 s          448 s           950 s          2.12x
--   clippy      584 s         1151 s          2600 s          2.26x  (timeout unchanged)
--   test-core   907 s         1656 s          3400 s          2.05x
--   text-gates  713 s          874 s          1800 s          2.06x  (timeout unchanged)
--
-- ci-spec is why this was not bookkeeping. Its timeout was 300 s against a 448 s worst, so the
-- ratio was 0.67 -- the gate could not finish inside its own limit, and 27 of its 115 runs
-- returned `gate_undecided`. Three consecutive undecideds exhaust the job, and a REQUIRED gate
-- that is exhausted leaves the roll-up permanently undecided: `gatehouse/required` then sits
-- in_progress until the controller's five-hour timeout ends it as `failure`. Every red required
-- context on nucleus that day was that, not a disagreement -- a whole day of the merge queue,
-- caused by a declared number that a real run had quietly outgrown. fmt (1.45x) and test-core
-- (1.27x) were under the 2x floor for the same reason and would have followed.
--
-- A CHANGE HERE DOES NOT REACH WORK ALREADY QUEUED. The controller serves the lane a plan
-- that is UPLOADED to it, not read from the tree under test, and a gate job binds its recipe
-- at ENQUEUE. Measured 2026-09-21: a ci-spec attempt retried an hour after a corrected plan
-- was uploaded still ran with --timeout-seconds 300, the old value. So a timeout fix takes
-- effect for requests made after the upload and for no others, and a tree already in the
-- queue keeps whatever limit it was enqueued with until it is re-requested. Worth knowing
-- before concluding from a red gate that the declaration below is still wrong.
--
-- test-audit's measurement is PROVISIONAL and this is the correction of a wrong reading.
--
-- The gate is required and had never executed once, because the plan the controller served
-- carried six gates rather than seven and nothing compares the two. Activating it on 2026-09-22
-- produced a run of 288800 ms which I recorded as confirming the 300000 ms estimate. That was
-- wrong: the run `decided`, and a decision can be FAILED. It was. 288800 ms is how long the gate
-- takes to fail during the build, not how long it takes to pass -- `decided` is not `held`, and
-- reading one as the other is the same error this file's own gates exist to prevent.
--
-- Why it failed: test-audit had NO STEPS. It ran `cargo nextest` as its cmd and so never ran
-- `scripts/gatehouse-verifier-sdk.sh`, which builds the wasm-pack output that
-- `nucleus-verifier-service/build.rs` requires; `cargo nextest -E <filter>` still BUILDS the
-- whole workspace, the filter only selects what to RUN. It now has the same two steps and the
-- same wasm tools test-core has.
--
-- 900000 ms WAS an ANCHOR: test-core does the same SDK build and full workspace build plus 7610
-- tests and had measured 821-907 s, so test-audit's 365 tests were expected to land near it. The
-- clause held on an estimate, which this file elsewhere calls a hope, correctly.
--
-- 1493565 ms REPLACES it, and the road to that number is the point of this comment.
--
-- FIRST ATTEMPT, and it was wrong. Four runs of the two-step gate between 13:29Z and 14:29Z on
-- 2026-09-22 measured 701925, 707056, 725281 and 776241 ms, and 776241 was declared as "the
-- worst run seen here" per the rule above. It was beaten inside two hours, by 1.9x: a fifth run
-- at 16:14Z took 1493565 ms. So the sample of four was not a measurement either -- it was a
-- quieter hope than the 900000 anchor it replaced, and WORSE than it, because the anchor was at
-- least conservative. This file's own sentence -- "a worst that a later run beats is not a
-- measurement, it is a hope" -- has now caught two consecutive declarations for this gate.
--
-- WHAT THE FIFTH RUN HAD THAT THE OTHER FOUR DID NOT: contention. The four quiet runs were taken
-- with ONE live tree in the lane. The 1493565 ms run was taken minutes after fourteen closed
-- pull requests were reopened, putting fifteen live trees on a lane whose policy is proved for
-- two commits in flight. That is not a reason to discard it. A timeout exists for exactly the
-- moment the builder is busy, so the contended number is the one that decides whether a
-- REQUIRED gate exhausts -- and 55% of its then-limit is how close it came.
--
-- The estimate's original reasoning still holds and now reads differently: 776 s against
-- test-core's 821-907 s looked like agreement, but those test-core figures were themselves
-- quiet-lane numbers. Nearly all of this gate is compilation (the 88% figure recorded above),
-- and compilation is exactly what contends.
--
-- THE TIMEOUT RISES TO 3600000, which is 2.41x, and that is the MOST the budget allows rather
-- than a convention. 2x1493565 = 2987130, so the old 2700000 is now below the floor and had to
-- move regardless. Measured against the kernel: 3600000 proves, 3800000 is REFUSED --
-- `builderBudget_b` binds between them, so the six required gates declare 12800000 ms and there
-- is roughly 200000 ms of slack left in the whole pipeline. Every other gate here sits near
-- 2.1x; this one takes the extra 0.3x because its sample is five runs from one day with a 2.1x
-- spread between smallest and largest, and because an exhausted required gate leaves
-- `gatehouse/required` undecided until the controller's five-hour timeout -- the ci-spec outage
-- recorded above. The asymmetry buys the margin; the budget caps it.
--
-- THE HONEST STATE: five observations is still not a sample, and the next re-measure should come
-- from a window that contains both a quiet lane and a contended one rather than from whichever
-- happened to be running. Until the lane has a priced, bounded notion of how many trees may be
-- live at once, "measured on the builder" does not name a single operating regime, and no
-- declaration here can be stable. That is a gap in the policy, not in the number.
--
-- lean-build's 145 s is still a CONTAINER measurement, because the lane cannot run it yet; it is
-- declared and not required, and the figure is marked here rather than trusted. text-gates took
-- FIVE seconds in a container and 874 in the microVM: the difference is the VM, not the greps,
-- and it is why a measurement taken anywhere else is not the measurement this clause wants.
--
-- The six required gates now declare 12800000 ms against a 21600000 ms window. The "94% of what
-- `builderBudget_b` allows" this paragraph used to claim was computed before `builders` went
-- from 1 to 2 in the same change, which doubled the basis; rather than restate a percentage
-- beside the clause that decides it -- one fact written twice, and the second copy drifted
-- within one commit -- what is recorded here is the kernel's own answer, measured by bisection:
-- test-audit at 3600000 PROVES and at 3800000 is REFUSED, so the pipeline has on the order of
-- 200000 ms of slack in total. That is tight where it used to be roomy, and it is the honest
-- cost of timeouts that fit the work: the room was never there, it was borrowed from
-- measurements that no longer held. Two commits in flight is the number the builder is proved
-- for; the day this was first measured had ELEVEN, and the day it was corrected had FIFTEEN,
-- which no timeout can fix.
-- THE POLICY NO LONGER DESCRIBES A MACHINE (gatehouse #81, adopted with #201). Ten of its twenty
-- fields were claims about the builder -- builders 2, builderDiskMb 32768, the gate tools
-- inventory, builderMemMb 8192, platform linux/arm64 (linux/amd64 since 2026-09-30), hostDiskMb 197316, hostMemMb 23981, lanes 2,
-- preparedMb 4096, the pinned seed `main-ebebf53cd-u@sha256:e8191022…` and a huge-page pool of
-- 8192 -- and every one of them was the operator's word. They leave the plan and become what
-- EACH LANE attests in its own `ci.Deployment` (`gate plan runnable`), which is also the only way
-- two lanes of different architectures can hold one plan: the arm64 lane and the x86_64 lane do
-- not have one memory size, one disk or one image between them. The numbers above are kept
-- because they are how the arm64 lane measured; they are no longer proved against from here.
--
-- `undecidedLimitMs` is 0, "no enforced limit declared", which is what the plan said before the
-- field existed. The controller's five-hour undecided timeout is SHORTER than `queueBudgetMs`,
-- so declaring it would make `deadlineFits_b` refuse this plan -- which is a real finding about
-- the budget and not one this change should paper over by picking a number.
let policy : ci.Policy = (ceiling, [#b"fmt", #b"clippy", #b"test-node", #b"test-libs", #b"ci-spec", #b"text-gates"] : Bytes, 21600000, 64, 2, 3, 10240, [#b"Rustfmt", #b"Clippy"] : Bytes, [#b"awk"] : Bytes, 0)

let admissible : So (ci.admissible_b pipeline policy) = oh
